WEBVTT

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.044
Welcome to our Education Support Staff
Professional Learning

00:00:08.044 --> 00:00:08.473
Workshop.

00:00:08.473 --> 00:00:11.240
Today we're going to explore Autistic
experience

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.961
and what it means for access, belonging
and partnership across

00:00:14.961 --> 00:00:15.801
the school day.

00:00:15.801 --> 00:00:19.255
Keep the students you work with at the
centre of the day.

00:00:19.255 --> 00:00:22.652
We'll build our understanding, look
closely at everyday school

00:00:22.652 --> 00:00:23.148
routines

00:00:23.148 --> 00:00:26.697
and consider what we can change in the
environment, the task and

00:00:26.697 --> 00:00:28.147
the support we offer.

00:00:28.147 --> 00:00:30.743
Our starting point is curiosity.

00:00:31.105 --> 00:00:34.006
We are here to understand students and
make participation more

00:00:34.006 --> 00:00:34.674
workable.

00:00:34.674 --> 00:00:38.013
We value students as they are and support
their participation

00:00:38.013 --> 00:00:39.025
and belonging.

00:00:39.025 --> 00:00:41.772
You don't need to arrive with all the
answers.

00:00:41.772 --> 00:00:44.816
Bring your questions, your experience and
a willingness to look

00:00:44.816 --> 00:00:45.169
again.

00:00:47.121 --> 00:00:52.043
I acknowledge the Traditional Custodians
of the lands on which we are learning

00:00:52.044 --> 00:00:57.158
today, and pay my respects to Elders past
and present. I recognise the continuing

00:00:57.159 --> 00:01:01.698
connections of Aboriginal and Torres
Strait Islander peoples to Country,

00:01:01.699 --> 00:01:06.493
culture and community. I extend that
respect to Aboriginal and Torres Strait

00:01:06.494 --> 00:01:09.689
Islander people taking part in this
learning today.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.780
Our Statement of Reconciliation is in your
workbook for you to read.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.920
For the sake of this demonstration, I'll
read it aloud.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.880
We at Positive Partnerships value and
recognise the diversity of

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.320
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander
cultures and heritage

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.520
and their proud part in Australia’s
national identity.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:34.530
We acknowledge and continue to learn about
Australia’s colonial

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:39.640
past and the historic and ongoing impacts
on Australia’s First Nations people.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.270
We believe in and strongly support the
Truth-telling of

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.140
Australia’s colonial history

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.850
and in moving forward as a nation we must
encourage open,

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:54.280
honest dialogue and build relationships
built on respect and trust.

00:01:56.681 --> 00:02:01.073
First Nations people are advised that this
workshop may contain images and

00:02:01.074 --> 00:02:03.599
voices of people who are no longer with
us.

00:02:05.601 --> 00:02:10.901
Everyone is welcome here, and you are
welcome to be authentically yourself. Use

00:02:10.902 --> 00:02:16.066
the strategies that help you take part.
You can move, change position, write,

00:02:16.067 --> 00:02:20.891
speak, listen or pass. If there is
something the delivery team can do to

00:02:20.892 --> 00:02:26.192
make participation more accessible, let us
know. Let's begin by getting to know

00:02:26.193 --> 00:02:31.425
the people around us. At your table, share
your first name, your role, and one

00:02:31.426 --> 00:02:36.183
hope you have for today. Keep the
introduction brief so everyone has an

00:02:36.184 --> 00:02:41.619
opportunity. You're also welcome simply to
listen. There is no need to disclose a

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:46.785
diagnosis or share private information
about yourself, a student or a school.

00:02:46.786 --> 00:02:51.950
What would make this learning useful in
your work? Take a moment to introduce

00:02:51.951 --> 00:02:52.968
yourselves now.

00:03:01.001 --> 00:03:05.456
Let's come back together. Keep that hope
in mind as we go. There will be

00:03:05.457 --> 00:03:10.540
opportunities to connect the content with
sensory experience, thinking, the school

00:03:10.541 --> 00:03:14.996
day, learning design and partnership. Your
questions are useful starting

00:03:14.997 --> 00:03:18.321
points, even when they don't have an
immediate answer.

00:03:19.841 --> 00:03:23.604
Before we get into the content, take a
moment to check the practical

00:03:23.605 --> 00:03:28.154
arrangements for your setting: the exits,
bathroom facilities, tea and coffee, and

00:03:28.155 --> 00:03:32.536
any quiet or lower-sensory space that is
available. Keep your Participant Guide

00:03:32.537 --> 00:03:36.917
and something to write with nearby. We'll
use the guide throughout the day, and

00:03:36.918 --> 00:03:41.074
there will be time to provide feedback
through the evaluation. You can also

00:03:41.075 --> 00:03:45.343
write questions down if speaking to the
group isn't your preferred way to ask

00:03:45.344 --> 00:03:49.725
them. Please be considerate with phone
sounds and calls, and follow the venue's

00:03:49.726 --> 00:03:54.107
arrangements for no smoking or vaping.
Attend to your own needs as we work. You

00:03:54.108 --> 00:03:58.151
don't need to wait for a scheduled break
to use a strategy that helps you

00:03:58.152 --> 00:04:01.913
participate. Confirm any local
arrangements with your delivery team.

00:04:03.441 --> 00:04:08.214
Turn to page four of your Participant
Guide. Bring two or three students to

00:04:08.215 --> 00:04:13.181
mind. Choose students whose experiences
differ in some way: perhaps their age,

00:04:13.182 --> 00:04:18.149
how they communicate, or what different
parts of school are like for them. Use

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:22.665
initials or a role description in your
notes. We won't need identifying

00:04:22.666 --> 00:04:27.761
information. Begin with the whole person.
What interests them? What strengths do

00:04:27.762 --> 00:04:32.471
you notice? How do they connect with
people or with the things they enjoy?

00:04:32.472 --> 00:04:37.438
Then ask yourself: what am I here to
understand more deeply about their school

00:04:37.439 --> 00:04:42.148
experience? Keep that as an open question,
rather than deciding the answer

00:04:42.149 --> 00:04:46.793
now. This is private thinking first.
Sharing is optional. We'll return to

00:04:46.794 --> 00:04:51.696
these same students when we map the school
day, consider learning design, and

00:04:51.697 --> 00:04:56.792
reflect on what to carry forward. Give
yourself a moment to choose your students

00:04:56.793 --> 00:04:58.146
and make a few notes.

00:05:06.161 --> 00:05:10.883
Keep those notes available. You don't need
a complete picture yet. The gaps in

00:05:10.884 --> 00:05:15.238
what we know are part of the work. As we
move through the workshop, keep

00:05:15.239 --> 00:05:19.654
distinguishing what a student has
communicated, what you've observed, and

00:05:19.655 --> 00:05:22.412
what you'd still like to understand with
them.

00:05:23.921 --> 00:05:28.677
We're going to begin with student voices.
Listen to each speaker's own account, and

00:05:28.678 --> 00:05:33.261
allow it to stand on its own terms. You
don't need to diagnose, compare speakers

00:05:33.262 --> 00:05:37.554
with students you know, or reduce the
video to one lesson. Just notice what

00:05:37.555 --> 00:05:41.906
stays with you, and what you want to
understand more deeply. We'll have time

00:05:41.907 --> 00:05:43.470
to reflect after the video.

00:08:05.881 --> 00:08:09.781
Take a moment before moving into
discussion. Keep the speakers' own words

00:08:09.782 --> 00:08:12.380
and experiences at the centre of your
reflection.

00:08:15.401 --> 00:08:20.290
Stay on page four of your Participant
Guide. What did you notice? What has

00:08:20.291 --> 00:08:25.314
stayed with you? What would you like to
understand more deeply? Begin with a

00:08:25.315 --> 00:08:30.606
little private writing. Anchor your notes
in what the speakers said, rather than

00:08:30.607 --> 00:08:35.430
making an interpretation from their
appearance or behaviour. Then, if you

00:08:35.431 --> 00:08:40.387
wish, share one reflection with someone
nearby. Try to leave each speaker's

00:08:40.388 --> 00:08:45.612
account intact. We don't need to reach
agreement about a single message, and we

00:08:45.613 --> 00:08:50.502
don't need to compare a speaker with one
particular child. Make room for a

00:08:50.503 --> 00:08:55.459
question that remains open as well as
something that feels clear. Take some

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:57.335
time with those prompts now.

00:09:05.361 --> 00:09:10.177
Let's return. Keep one question from that
reflection where you can see it. We will

00:09:10.178 --> 00:09:14.340
come back to sensory experience, thinking,
communication and the school

00:09:14.341 --> 00:09:18.860
day. Some questions may need further
conversation through the program or your

00:09:18.861 --> 00:09:23.261
local channels. Listening carefully
includes recognising what we still need

00:09:23.262 --> 00:09:23.796
to learn.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:29.968
Let's take a moment with the language on
this slide, because it gives us a shared

00:09:29.968 --> 00:09:34.208
starting point. Neurodiversity is the
whole picture. It includes everyone.

00:09:34.208 --> 00:09:38.914
Neurotypical and neurodivergent describe
brain patterns within that diversity, and

00:09:38.914 --> 00:09:43.271
autism is one expression of it. Here,
neurotypical describes the more common

00:09:43.271 --> 00:09:47.744
brain patterns. Neurodivergent describes
brain patterns that differ from those

00:09:47.744 --> 00:09:52.160
most common patterns. So both sit within
the wider picture of neurodiversity.

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:56.517
Keeping those ideas clear helps us use the
language consistently through the

00:09:56.517 --> 00:10:00.990
day. Now, hold in mind the students you
were thinking about earlier. The words

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:05.521
can help us ask better questions about
their experience, but they won't tell us

00:10:05.521 --> 00:10:09.530
everything about any one of them. We still
need to know that student's

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:14.003
interests, their ways of communicating,
and what different parts of school are

00:10:14.003 --> 00:10:18.418
like for them. We'll also respect the
language people use for themselves. The

00:10:18.418 --> 00:10:22.834
purpose here is to support dignity and
curiosity. As we go, keep asking: does

00:10:22.834 --> 00:10:26.201
this language help me understand the
person in front of me?

00:10:27.721 --> 00:10:32.326
That brings us to what it means to be
neurodiversity affirming. The starting

00:10:32.327 --> 00:10:36.626
point is that differences are not
deficits. There are different ways of

00:10:36.627 --> 00:10:41.416
knowing, doing, being and belonging, and
everyone has strengths and experiences

00:10:41.417 --> 00:10:46.022
challenges. That means allowing for
different ways of thinking, learning and

00:10:46.023 --> 00:10:50.690
taking part. When we look at belonging,
for instance, we can stay open to the

00:10:50.691 --> 00:10:55.542
ways a student experiences connection,
rather than expecting it to look the same

00:10:55.543 --> 00:11:00.149
for everyone. In a primary classroom, it's
easy for the things that need our

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:04.816
immediate attention to fill the whole
picture. A difficult transition. A task

00:11:04.817 --> 00:11:09.238
that hasn't started. A moment when joining
the group has become too much.

00:11:09.239 --> 00:11:13.599
Those things matter. We need to understand
them and provide support. And

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:18.451
we need to keep the rest of the person
visible. What interests them? What brings

00:11:18.452 --> 00:11:23.303
them joy? How do they connect? What can
they do that we might miss if we're only

00:11:23.304 --> 00:11:28.216
watching for difficulty? Take one of your
students now. What becomes more visible

00:11:28.217 --> 00:11:32.884
when you include their strengths and
interests in the picture? Just hold that

00:11:32.885 --> 00:11:34.174
thought for a moment.

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:47.441
A strengths-based approach also takes
difficulty seriously. A student can have

00:11:47.442 --> 00:11:52.888
real strengths and still need substantial
support. Their capacity can change with

00:11:52.889 --> 00:11:58.471
the task, the environment and the day. So
our language needs to make room for both.

00:11:58.472 --> 00:12:03.236
We can describe what's becoming difficult,
notice what the situation is

00:12:03.237 --> 00:12:08.479
asking of the student, and consider what
support would help. We begin with the

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:11.270
person, and we stay curious about access.

00:12:12.801 --> 00:12:17.689
The social model helps us put that
curiosity to work. On this slide, we're

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:23.046
using an environment-first lens. We look
for barriers in the place, the task, the

00:12:23.047 --> 00:12:28.136
routine and the expectations. Then we ask
what can change around the student.

00:12:28.137 --> 00:12:33.494
Here's an imaginary classroom example. A
class is gathering for a group activity.

00:12:33.495 --> 00:12:38.651
The usual expectation is that everyone
sits close together, in the same space,

00:12:38.652 --> 00:12:43.673
for the whole activity. One student finds
that arrangement difficult. Before

00:12:43.674 --> 00:12:48.629
deciding what the student needs to do
differently, we can look at what this

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:54.054
routine asks of them. How crowded is the
space? What choices are there about where

00:12:54.055 --> 00:12:58.876
to sit? Is sitting in that particular
place necessary to take part in the

00:12:58.877 --> 00:13:04.099
activity? We might discuss another place
to sit, or a different way of joining,

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:09.189
with the student and teacher. Those are
possibilities to investigate with the

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:14.480
student. We haven't established what will
help just by coming up with an option.

00:13:14.481 --> 00:13:19.704
The next two steps on the slide matter:
include the student's communication and

00:13:19.705 --> 00:13:25.061
experience, and review whether the change
makes participation more workable. What

00:13:25.062 --> 00:13:30.285
can the student tell or show us about the
change? Is it helping them take part?

00:13:30.286 --> 00:13:35.174
Changing an environment won't make every
difficulty disappear. It gives us

00:13:35.175 --> 00:13:40.531
somewhere useful to begin, with barriers
and supports that we can notice, discuss

00:13:40.532 --> 00:13:41.268
and review.

00:13:42.801 --> 00:13:47.253
Let's widen that question to school
itself. You can use the space on page

00:13:47.254 --> 00:13:52.016
five of your Participant Guide as you
think. What is school for? Who is school

00:13:52.017 --> 00:13:56.346
for? How do students learn? And whose ways
of communicating, moving and

00:13:56.347 --> 00:14:01.170
participating are treated as expected?
These are big questions. There isn't one

00:14:01.171 --> 00:14:06.056
correct answer we're trying to arrive at.
They're an invitation to look again at

00:14:06.057 --> 00:14:10.757
familiar routines and the assumptions
inside them. Think about a routine in a

00:14:10.758 --> 00:14:15.334
setting where you work: perhaps arriving
in the morning, joining a group or

00:14:15.335 --> 00:14:19.911
showing what you've learnt. What does that
routine seem to assume about how

00:14:19.912 --> 00:14:24.736
students will communicate or participate?
What do we notice and encourage? What

00:14:24.737 --> 00:14:29.560
might be harder to see? This is a chance
to examine the routine with curiosity.

00:14:29.561 --> 00:14:34.261
Every classroom has patterns that have
become familiar. Looking at them again

00:14:34.262 --> 00:14:38.838
can open up choices. We can value the care
and effort that educators bring,

00:14:38.839 --> 00:14:43.353
while still asking whether a familiar
expectation is helping every student

00:14:43.354 --> 00:14:48.116
access the learning. For a moment, work
quietly on your own. Choose one of the

00:14:48.117 --> 00:14:52.446
four questions that catches your
attention, and jot down a thought or a

00:14:52.447 --> 00:14:54.362
question you'd like to explore.

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:07.093
Hold on to the question you've chosen. Our
assumptions influence what we notice,

00:15:07.094 --> 00:15:11.489
what we reward and what we think needs to
change. We'll now take that wider

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:16.064
reflection into one specific routine, so
the question becomes something we can

00:15:16.065 --> 00:15:17.074
examine together.

00:15:18.601 --> 00:15:23.685
Now turn to a small group near you. To
begin, take the question about whose ways

00:15:23.686 --> 00:15:27.934
of communicating, moving and participating
are treated as expected.

00:15:27.935 --> 00:15:32.826
Choose one familiar routine or decision to
explore together. Keep it specific

00:15:32.827 --> 00:15:37.846
enough to picture. You might choose how
students join a whole-class discussion,

00:15:37.847 --> 00:15:42.674
for example. Talk about the routine and
the system, without naming students,

00:15:42.675 --> 00:15:47.824
colleagues or schools. Then work through
the prompts on screen. Which assumptions

00:15:47.825 --> 00:15:52.458
shape that routine? Who can access it
easily? Where might the environment

00:15:52.459 --> 00:15:57.222
create a barrier? How could curiosity
change the next response? If you find

00:15:57.223 --> 00:16:01.792
yourselves focusing mainly on what a
student does, come back to what the

00:16:01.793 --> 00:16:06.169
environment, task or expectation is
contributing. Before we come back

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:10.804
together, choose one concise observation
to share, and one example of how

00:16:10.805 --> 00:16:15.503
curiosity could change your next response.
Keep the prompts in view as you

00:16:15.504 --> 00:16:16.789
talk. You can begin.

00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:29.566
Let's come back together. Finish the
thought you're on, and keep your notes.

00:16:29.567 --> 00:16:34.333
If you're sharing with the group, offer
one concise observation: one example

00:16:34.334 --> 00:16:39.354
where curiosity could change your next
response. Keep the example focused on the

00:16:39.355 --> 00:16:44.375
routine, the task or the environment. As
we close, keep that question about your

00:16:44.376 --> 00:16:49.142
next response with you. The shift we're
practising is from assumptions about

00:16:49.143 --> 00:16:53.718
students towards questions about access,
environment and learning design.

00:16:53.719 --> 00:16:58.358
Curiosity gives us time to look at the
task and the setting before placing

00:16:58.359 --> 00:17:03.379
another demand on a student. It helps us
identify something to explore, with the

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:08.400
student's experience still at the centre.
Keep your example available as we move

00:17:08.401 --> 00:17:08.591
on.

00:17:10.120 --> 00:17:15.582
The Disability Standards for Education
2005 are part of the context for our work

00:17:15.583 --> 00:17:20.906
on access and adjustments. There are three
Australian Government Department of

00:17:20.907 --> 00:17:25.815
Education resources to be aware of
alongside this workshop. The first is

00:17:25.816 --> 00:17:31.347
Getting started. The second is Advocating
with and for your child: A workbook for

00:17:31.348 --> 00:17:36.671
parents and carers. That workbook is
written for families, so it is useful for

00:17:36.672 --> 00:17:42.203
educators to know what parents and carers
may be reading and working through. The

00:17:42.204 --> 00:17:47.182
third resource is Milestones and
transitions. The links are listed in the

00:17:47.183 --> 00:17:52.783
facilitator resources. Use the published
materials to follow up, and take detailed

00:17:52.784 --> 00:17:58.383
or school-specific questions through the
appropriate program or local channel. For

00:17:58.384 --> 00:18:03.431
our work today, keep the connection with
access in view: understanding the

00:18:03.432 --> 00:18:08.548
student, looking closely at the demands of
the setting, and considering the

00:18:08.549 --> 00:18:12.766
adjustments and support that make
participation more workable.

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:19.387
Autism can be understood as difference and
as a dynamic disability. Holding both

00:18:19.388 --> 00:18:24.300
ideas matters. Recognising difference and
strengths does not mean overlooking

00:18:24.301 --> 00:18:29.150
difficulty or the need for substantial
support. Access and support needs can

00:18:29.151 --> 00:18:34.193
change with the task, the environment,
relationships and the capacity available

00:18:34.194 --> 00:18:39.430
at that time. Something a student can do
in one setting, or on one day, may not be

00:18:39.431 --> 00:18:44.537
available in another. We need to look at
what is happening around the student as

00:18:44.538 --> 00:18:49.774
well as what the task is asking of them.
That takes us away from a fixed judgement

00:18:49.775 --> 00:18:54.623
about what someone can always do. It
invites a more useful question: what is

00:18:54.624 --> 00:18:59.472
making participation possible here, and
what support is needed now? Keep the

00:18:59.473 --> 00:19:04.321
individual in view. There isn't one
pattern that will explain every Autistic

00:19:04.322 --> 00:19:09.041
student's experience. Strengths,
difficulties and fluctuating capacity all

00:19:09.042 --> 00:19:13.955
belong in the picture we build with the
student and the people who know them.

00:19:15.481 --> 00:19:20.113
Here is where the day is taking us. We'll
develop a neurodiversity affirming

00:19:20.114 --> 00:19:24.622
approach, deepen our understanding of
Autistic experience, notice how that

00:19:24.623 --> 00:19:29.440
experience meets the school day, consider
accessible learning environments, and

00:19:29.441 --> 00:19:34.011
work in partnership. The first two
sessions build our shared understanding.

00:19:34.012 --> 00:19:38.829
Then we'll bring that understanding into a
school day, in primary and secondary

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:43.400
settings, and into practical choices about
the support you give. Belonging,

00:19:43.401 --> 00:19:48.403
capacity and joy stay with us throughout.
The workshop schedule allows for morning

00:19:48.404 --> 00:19:53.098
tea from ten forty-five to eleven, and
lunch from twelve thirty to one. Treat

00:19:53.099 --> 00:19:58.039
those as the planned timings and follow
the arrangements for your session. Before

00:19:58.040 --> 00:20:03.043
we move on, turn to My starting point on
page five of your Participant Guide. Make

00:20:03.044 --> 00:20:08.046
a brief note of where you are beginning: a
question, a hope or an idea you want to

00:20:08.047 --> 00:20:12.864
examine. We will return to this in our
final reflection. Take a moment to write

00:20:12.865 --> 00:20:13.110
now.

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:25.542
Keep that starting point. You don't have
to resolve it yet. Next we'll explore

